---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import LeadForm from "../components/LeadForm.astro";
import CallLink from "../components/CallLink.astro";

const title = "Water damage restoration in Dacula, GA";
const description =
  "Burst pipe, leak, or storm water in Dacula, GA 30019. Call or send what happened.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="hero wrap">
    <p class="muted">Gwinnett County</p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Water on the floor in Dacula? Call or send the form. Tell us your street
      and ZIP. Dacula covers old town, Harbins, and Hamilton Mill, and they are
      not all the same kind of house.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn" href="#request">Request help in Dacula</a>
      <CallLink class="btn secondary" />
      <a class="btn secondary" href="/what-to-do">First-hour checklist</a>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Water damage in Dacula</h2>
    <p>
      Most homes in Dacula were built in the late 1990s and early 2000s. That is
      a good age for a house, but it is also the age when the water heater, the
      ice-maker line, and the washer hose start to fail.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the usual call is not a flood. It is a wet garage, a soft kitchen
      floor, or a stain on the ceiling. A burst pipe or a leaking appliance does
      most of the damage here.
    </p>
    <p>
      What is under your floor matters. Many homes near Apalachee Crossing and
      in Hamilton Mill have a finished basement. Older houses in old town,
      around Broad Street and Fence Road, usually have a crawl space. A leak
      upstairs can end up in the basement, and a leak over a crawl space has to
      be dried from underneath. Either way, the water has to be removed and the
      structure has to dry before mold can start.
    </p>
    <p>
      Storms cause less damage here than leaks do, but they still do some. A
      hard rain can push water in at a window, a sliding door, or a bad spot in
      the roof. Wind and falling trees do the same. Dacula had trees down in a
      January 2022 winter storm, and hail the size of baseballs fell nearby in
      2018. Water gets in through damaged shingles and flashing, and the stain
      on the ceiling can show up days later.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Where people say they live</h2>
    <p>Dacula is a few different places that share one ZIP code, 30019.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Old Dacula.</strong> Start at Fence Road and Dacula Road, by the plaza
      and the post office. Broad Street runs through the old town. The houses are
      a mix of ages.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Off Highway 316.</strong> Apalachee Farms is here. Club View, Sterling
      Green, Riverside, The Terraces, and The Oaks (by the golf course) were built
      from about 1995 to 2006. Apalachee Crossing is next to it, from the early 2000s.
      Daniel Park, Belfaire, and Ewing Estates are from the same years.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Harbins.</strong> Head the other way for bigger lots. Harbins Estates
      and Harbins Plantation are a little older. Preserve at Harbins Ridge is newer.
      The mailbox still says Dacula.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Hamilton Mill.</strong> This is the I-85 side, at Exit 120. People who
      live there say Hamilton Mill or "HM." It has the same ZIP, but it is not the
      same neighborhood as Broad Street.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Common causes in Dacula</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Burst pipe.</strong> After a hard freeze, a pipe can split in a wall
        or an attic. Gwinnett had a lot of these in December 2022.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Water heater.</strong> The tank leaks or lets go in a garage or closet.
        This is common in houses from the late 1990s and early 2000s.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Refrigerator or washer line.</strong> The hose cracks. Water slides
        under the floor, and you smell it before you see it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A leak that reaches the basement.</strong> Water from upstairs runs
        down into a finished basement. Basement flood cleanup takes longer than a
        one-room leak.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Storm water.</strong> Rain gets in through a window, a sliding door,
        or a roof damaged by wind or a falling tree.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sewage, a sagging ceiling, or wet outlets.</strong> Do not clean these
        up yourself. Call.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>If water is in the house now</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Shut the water off, if you can do it safely. Stay away from wet outlets.</li>
      <li>Take photos of the water, the room, and where it started.</li>
      <li>
        Sewage, a sagging ceiling, or wet outlets: <CallLink prefix="call" /> or
        <a href="#request">send the form</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Already stopped a small drip? Use the
        <a href="/small-leaks">small-leak steps</a>.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Call your insurance company when you can. If you plan to file a claim, do
      not start cutting out drywall first.
    </p>
    <p>
      Do not wait until tomorrow to dry things out. Wet drywall and flooring get
      worse overnight, and mold can start in 24 to 48 hours.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <h2>Questions from Dacula homeowners</h2>
    <dl class="faq">
      <div>
        <dt>
          My water heater leaked in the garage and the floor looks dry now. Do I
          still need to call?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Check before you decide. Water from a water heater runs under the wall
          and into the bottom of the drywall. Press on the baseboard and the
          lower wall. Look at the carpet and pad in the room next to the garage.
          If anything is soft, stained, or smells damp, call. If it only hit
          bare concrete and is dry, you probably caught it in time.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          Water from upstairs is coming through my basement ceiling. What do I
          do?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Shut off the water if you can. Then stay out of the basement if the
          ceiling is sagging, a light fixture is dripping, or the floor is wet
          near outlets. Water can follow wiring and pool in the drywall above
          you. Take photos, then call. Finished basements take longer to dry,
          because the carpet, pad, and walls all hold water.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Rain is coming in at my sliding door. Is that water damage?</dt>
        <dd>
          Yes. Water that comes in through a door, a window, or the roof can
          soak the floor and the wall under it. Take photos, put towels down,
          and call if it reached carpet, wood, or drywall. Storm water is often
          treated differently than a burst pipe in an insurance policy, so ask
          your insurer about it.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>My house was built around 2000 off 316. What usually breaks?</dt>
        <dd>
          The water heater, the refrigerator line, and the washer hose. If the
          water is off and you can see where it dripped, start with the
          <a href="/small-leaks">small-leak steps</a>. If the floor is still
          wet, call.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>
          It is 2 a.m. and the leak is stopped. Should I call now or wait until
          morning?
        </dt>
        <dd>
          Call now if water is still coming in, there is sewage, or the ceiling
          is sagging. If the leak is stopped and it is one small area, take
          photos, pick up what you can, and call first thing in the morning. Do
          not leave wet carpet and drywall for days.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Can I stay in my house?</dt>
        <dd>
          Often yes, if it is a clean-water leak in one room. Stay out if there
          is sewage, a wet electrical panel, or a ceiling that is coming down.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Questions about drying time, insurance, or whether you have to hire the
      company that calls are answered on <a href="/faq">FAQ</a> and
      <a href="/insurance">Insurance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap">
    <p>
      <strong>What a job looks like.</strong> A crew checks the house, pulls out
      standing water, dries the structure, and replaces what can't be saved. You
      should be shown moisture readings, not just told it looks dry. How long it
      takes depends on the job. <a href="/how-it-works">How it works</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Clean water, dirty water, sewage.</strong> A broken supply line is
      not the same as a washer overflow or a sewer backup. Say which one it is on
      the form.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Insurance.</strong> A sudden burst pipe or a failed appliance is often
      covered. A slow leak, creek water, or sewage may not be unless you bought extra
      coverage. Take photos first. Don't tear out drywall to "help." Ask your insurer.
      <a href="/insurance">Insurance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;" id="request">
    <h2>Request help</h2>
    <p class="lede">
      Name, phone, city, ZIP, and what happened. Someone will call you back.
    </p>
    <LeadForm defaultCity="dacula" />
  </section>

  <section class="wrap" style="padding-bottom: 2rem;">
    <h2>Nearby</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/lawrenceville">Lawrenceville</a> ·
      <a href="/grayson">Grayson</a> ·
      <a href="/loganville">Loganville</a> ·
      <a href="/winder">Winder</a> ·
      <a href="/gwinnett-county">Gwinnett County</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
